<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psalm 137</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">during or after the Babylonian exile (deportations from <a href="/years/597-bce">597 BCE</a>; Babylon fell in <a href="/years/539-bce">539 BCE</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Super flumina Babylonis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 597 BC, and in the campaigns that followed, people of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, where they were held until some time after the fall of Babylon in 539 BC. Psalm 137 gives voice to that exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars compare it with the biblical Book of Lamentations on the fall of Jerusalem in the sixth century BC, which is similar in style and theme, and with laments in Jeremiah, Ezekiel and <b>Psalm 137</b>; Philip S. Alexander has set some of its lines beside Lamentations 2:17.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Psalm 137</b> gives voice to that exile.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a></li><li><blockquote>The libretto is by Temistocle Solera, who drew on <b>Psalm 137</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</a></li></ul></section>
